# MiniGolf - ComS 4370 Project 1A
# Author - Dylan Topic - Dylan004@iastate.edu
Mini golf game made with MonoGame for ComS 4370. Top down 2D game where you 
try to get the ball in the hole in as few shots as possible.

---

## How to run

Make sure you have .NET 9 installed, then just run:

    dotnet run

---

## Controls

- Mouse - aim your shot
- Left/Right arrow keys - fine tune the aim if you dont want to use the mouse
- Up/Down arrow keys - change shot power
- Space - shoot
- R - reset

---

## How to play

Move your mouse around the ball to aim, adjust power with up/down arrows, 
and hit space to shoot. Try to get the ball in the blue circle. Your stroke 
count is shown in the top left. Lower is better.

---

## Project structure

    MiniGolf/
     Components/
       BallComponent.cs      - the ball, handles drawing and tracks position/velocity
       CourseComponent.cs    - draws everything on the course (walls, hole, slope, etc)
       HUDComponent.cs       - draws the power bar, aim arrow, and stroke counter
       InputComponent.cs     - keyboard and mouse input
       PhysicsEngine.cs      - all the physics stuff (movement, collisions, slopes)
       SoundManager.cs       - handles sound effects
     Content/
       swing.wav             - plays when you hit the ball
       bounce.wav            - plays when ball hits a wall
       hole.wav              - plays when ball goes in the hole
       GameFont.spritefont   - font for the HUD
     Game1.cs                  - sets up and registers all the components
     Program.cs                - entry point

---

## The course

The course is an L-shaped hole. It has:

- outer walls that keep the ball in bounds
- inner walls that create corners and banks
- a blue rectangle at the bottom left thats the tee (starting position)
- a red slope region that pushes the ball downward when the ball rolls through it
- a dark gray obstacle block in the way
- a diagonal wall in the corner that bounces the ball at an angle
- a blue circle thats the hole

---

## Physics

I wrote a custom 2D physics engine for this. Here is what it does:

- gives the ball a velocity when you shoot based on angle and power
- applies friction every frame so the ball slows down and stops
- splits movement into 5 substeps per frame so the ball doesnt clip through walls
- handles rectangle collisions with all the walls and obstacles
- handles the diagonal wall using line segment vs circle math
- applies a force to the ball when it passes over the slope region
- detects when the ball is close enough to the hole to sink it

---

## Extra credit

I added the slope arrow grid feature. The red slope area is divided into a 
grid of small squares and each one has an arrow showing which direction the 
ball will get pushed. The arrows point in the direction of the slope force 
vector from the physics engine.

---

## Notes

- only one hole, no multiple hole progression
- R resets the ball back to the tee and resets the stroke count
- the diagonal wall collision uses a different collision method than the 
  regular walls since normal rectangle collision doesnt work for angles

---

